{"affected_paths": ["agent_cost/aggregate.py"], "claim_kind": "invariant", "conformance_status": "matched", "contradicting_refs": [], "epistemic_status": "single_source_observation", "observation_id": "OBS-010", "origin": "reconstructed", "provenance": [{"content_digest": "a2d26673aa9122f441c92c4d1fe7662bfa6dceda98a862ae7136114f45a8cb8c", "extraction_method": "static-read-source", "observed_at": "2026-08-15T00:00:00Z", "repo_commit": "1c74d44903e67e1f65bb28a0b0d39443f48f657b", "selector": "_STATUS_RANK", "source_kind": "source", "uri": "agent_cost/aggregate.py"}], "review_status": "unreviewed", "revision_digest": "9dcc3ff1b4c76363ba5ab72e8429006974d0ac62c1a59ebb796cf8bede619089", "statement": "build_rows()'s row.pricing_status ranking is defined in code as a full three-way order -- unpriced (rank 0) beats lower_bound (rank 1) beats priced (rank 2), via _STATUS_RANK -- so a row containing both a lower_bound fact and a plain priced fact should end up \"lower_bound\", not \"priced\". Only the unpriced-over-priced half of this ordering is exercised by an existing test (test_build_rows_mixed_priced_and_unpriced_marks_row_unpriced); no test in tests/test_aggregate.py builds a row from a mix of a lower_bound fact and a priced fact, so the lower_bound-vs-priced half of the ranking is confirmed only by reading _STATUS_RANK and build_rows() directly, not by execution.", "subject_refs": ["build_rows", "_STATUS_RANK"], "supporting_refs": [], "topic_id": "T-03", "valid_at_commit": "1c74d44903e67e1f65bb28a0b0d39443f48f657b"}
